### 11.6 Devices: one authoring contract, no taxonomy

FlightCore's input/output/GUI trichotomy is lock choreography, not modeling. With no lock, the protocol the taxonomy encoded has no referent (D-025). A device is an attached participant in the periphery (everything outside the loop that exchanges data with it). A device may be input-only or output-only, and those are degenerate uses, not framework classes (D-025). A bidirectional network peer is one device with one socket and one lifecycle, not two framework devices sharing state. The GUI is an ordinary device, the paradigm one, and it uses every capability of the handle below. Its one genuine peculiarity, read-modify-write widgets, is not taxonomic (§11.7).

#### The device handle

Every attached device receives the same handle. The handle carries the two primitive capabilities, read and stage, plus control access (observe running, request shutdown) (D-025). Read returns the latest snapshot (the immutable per-boundary publication). It can optionally wait for the next boundary (a published consistency point) first (§12.3).

Each attached device has a roster entry (its record in the list of attached devices). A handle outlives its roster entry as an object only (D-244). `detach!` retires the roster entry and the compiled writer behind it, but the caller may still hold the handle `attach!` returned. Its two write primitives, `stage!` and `report!`, then refuse by name (`DeviceContractMismatch`, Appendix C). A batch staged there would land in a staging cell, and no drain (the frame-top swap that applies staged device writes to the root inputs) reads that cell. A report would land in a diagnostic cell (each writer's bounded ring of diagnostics and its heartbeat). No frame top (the start of one loop iteration) folds that cell. Silence is the hidden-bug class this section tolerates nowhere. The read primitives stay legal, since they read the simulation's shared state and nothing of the roster entry's (D-244).

#### The authoring contract

A device is a user type subtyping the framework's neutral root, `MyDevice <: AbstractDevice` (D-177). That is one mandatory word, and it costs nothing, because the periphery has no competing hierarchy to inherit from. What it buys is `attach!`'s dispatch gate (D-177).

`attach!(sim::Simulation, dev::AbstractDevice, new_binding::AbstractBinding; should_abort::Bool = false)`

`attach!` takes the device with its binding (the value that makes a device framework-legible, described below). Its keyword `should_abort` (the per-attachment failure policy) is covered after the loop bodies below.

The framework asks for four functions.

`init!(dev) # per-run resource acquisition — calling task, before spawn (§12.4) loop(dev, handle) # the task body: owns its own wait structure shutdown!(dev) # per-run resource release — guaranteed on every exit path unblock!(dev) # make a blocked loop return (§12.4)`

Only `loop` is mandatory (D-366). `init!`, `shutdown!` and `unblock!` are optional hooks, each defaulting to a no-op. `attach!` refuses a device with no `loop` method (`DeviceContractMismatch`, D-216).

The framework owns everything around the four functions (D-102). The wrapper is the shutdown protocol (§12.4) made structural.

`init!(dev) # its own bracket, pre-spawn (§12.4): a throw # here is shutdown! + DeviceCrash by name task = Threads.@spawn try loop(dev, handle) catch e report!(entry, DeviceCrash(e)) # §12.4(6): sim continues, device absent finally shutdown!(dev) # any exit path: OS resources released # no dead-marking: task_state shows the end, claims stay, §11.3 end`

#### What `shutdown!` must tolerate

`shutdown!` must tolerate a partially initialized device (D-138). The release guarantee holds on the one path outside this wrapper too. The initialization step (§12.4) brackets each `init!` (D-138). A device that threw half-way through acquisition goes straight back to `shutdown!`, so nothing it did manage to open is leaked. The obligation that follows is "close only what is open". That is the same defensiveness `shutdown!` already owes the crash path, where a loop body may die at any point in its own life. `init!` is correspondingly not asked to clean up after itself. The bracket does once, for every device, what would otherwise be duplicated in each and enforced in none.

The same tolerance covers a repeated call (D-268). The wrapper runs `shutdown!` once on every exit path. When an operator interrupt (Ctrl-C in an interactive session) reaches `run!` before its tail, `run!` releases directly, not through a wrapper, each device it never spawned, advancing a cursor after each `shutdown!` returns, and retries from where an interrupt cut it (§12.4, D-268). An interrupt landing between a `shutdown!`'s return and the cursor's advance repeats that call rather than leak the device, so each such interrupt adds one call. A `shutdown!` that closes only what is open is idempotent by construction, so a repeated call costs nothing and is never reported.

The tolerance also covers a call with no `init!` before it (D-268). The initialization step lists each device for release before its `init!` begins, so that no interrupt can leave an initialized device unlisted (§12.4, D-268). An operator interrupt landing between the listing and the `init!` therefore releases a device that opened nothing. A `shutdown!` that closes only what is open does nothing there, and the call is never reported.

#### The operator interrupt

The wrapper makes one discrimination. An `InterruptException` is never a `DeviceCrash` (D-132). The operator interrupt is a control-plane stop, not a failure (D-132). The wrapper forwards the control-plane stop. There is no crash report for what is not a crash, and no `should_abort` consultation, since a stop is already requested.

#### The author-owned loop body

One device contract means author-owned loop bodies. A framework-owned hook loop would have to ask each device what it waits on, which is the rejected taxonomy resurrected as a trait (D-102). Under the author-owned body, every wait structure is ordinary user code composed from handle primitives. In the sketches, `T16000M` is FlightCore's type for the Thrustmaster T16000M joystick, and `UDPInput` is FlightCore's UDP input device type. The sketches map data through the binding's `map_input` and `map_output`, described below.

```julia function loop(dev::T16000M, handle) # timer-driven, full write-set while running(handle) sleep(dev.Δt_poll) stage!(handle, map_input(poll_axes(dev), binding(handle))...) end end

function loop(dev::UDPInput, handle) # source-driven, data-dependent while running(handle) datum = recv(dev.socket) # blocks; unblock! closes the socket is_eot(datum) && return # voluntary exit try stage!(handle, map_input(datum, binding(handle))...) catch e is_datum_error(dev, e) || rethrow() # a bug → wrapper → DeviceCrash report!(handle, MalformedDatum(e)) # garbage → visible, bounded, alive end end end

function loop(dev::Telemetry, handle) # boundary-driven output while running(handle) snap = wait_next_snapshot(handle) # §12.3; returns on stop send(dev.socket, map_output(gather(handle, snap), binding(handle))) end end ```

A bidirectional peer composes both halves itself, with an inner reader task inside its own domain, rather than forcing a select engine into the framework. Two idioms are author obligations the framework can only teach and diagnose, never force (Appendix A, D-102). The author must loop on `running(handle)` and must make blocking calls interruptible (`unblock!`, or timeouts). A forgotten predicate check surfaces as `DeviceJoinTimeout` with the device's name. A stall surfaces as a stale heartbeat (§12.2). Liveness timestamps ride inside the handle primitives, which store them in the device's own diagnostic cell (§11.8), so the framework observes activity without owning the loop.

FlightCore's device hook `should_close` dissolves (D-102). A window ✕ or peer EOT is the loop body returning. The wrapper's exit path releases the device's OS resources and consults `should_abort`. Nothing marks the device dead beyond its record. The task has ended, `task_state` says so at the next publication, and its heartbeat goes stale (§12.2). A device's claim is the set of faces (the names ports wear on their component's boundary) it may write. Claims and the roster entry persist to run end (the freeze, §11.3, D-106). This is §12.4(6)'s case. The GUI implements the same authoring contract.

`should_abort` is an `attach!` keyword, defaulting to `false` (D-138). It is per-attachment, never a device property. The same joystick is advisory in one deployment and decisive in another. With `should_abort` clear, a device's departure is reported and the run continues without it. With it set, that departure also requests a sim stop (§12.4). A departure is the loop body returning, a crash, or a failed `init!`. A hardware-in-the-loop rig attaches its plant interface with it set, since a run without that device is meaningless.

#### Bad datum versus bug: two classes, two fates

A datum that cannot be mapped for environmental reasons (a truncated datagram, malformed JSON, an out-of-range field) is a bad datum, tolerated in the loop body (D-105). The body catches it, stages nothing, calls `report!(handle, MalformedDatum(cause))` and continues. That tolerance is bounded by the device's own diagnostic cell (the ring and suppressed counts, §11.8; the stream, §13.2), and what it records is visible next to a live heartbeat. Any other exception propagates, and the wrapper turns it into `DeviceCrash` (§12.4).

The classification is the author's, because only they know their parser. FlightCore's `InputMappingError` docstring assigned it the same way. What changes under the author-owned loop is that no framework per-iteration catch site exists, so the framework's contribution is the diagnostic channel, not the catch. A marked exception type is not provided (D-105). `report!(handle, …)` writes device-attributed runtime warnings into that device's diagnostic cell, the single-writer entry point into the runtime warning stream (§13.2, §11.8, D-160), and nothing more. It is not a general user-diagnostics channel. Tolerating everything hides bugs as "device attached, nothing happens". Tolerating nothing kills a live telemetry link on its first truncated datagram, and since tasks are per-run artifacts (D-093), kills it for the rest of the run.
